\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Breaking the weights breaks the half-turn}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{In the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), take #{t,m>0}
and the [half-turn](fdip-000J). It fails to preserve the action after
a specific positive change of weights. Replace
#{(1,t,1,t)} by #{(1,t,2,t)} while keeping the proposed half-turn.
Then #{L_{00}=1+t} and #{L_{22}=2+t}, so #{PLP^T\ne L} and
#{\mathcal A(\delta_2)-\mathcal A(\delta_0)=1/2}.
If its Gaussian law were invariant, second moments would give
#{PGP^T=G}; inversion would give #{PQP^T=Q}, contradicting
those diagonal entries. At #{t=m=1}, retain the opposite pair
#{R=(0,2)} and eliminate #{D=(1,3)}. The blocks in that order are
##{\begin{aligned}
A=C&=\operatorname{diag}(3,4),\\
H&=-\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},\\
S_{\mathrm{broken}}
 &=\begin{pmatrix}29/12&-5/6\\-5/6&8/3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Indeed
##{HC^{-1}H^T=\begin{pmatrix}7/12&5/6\\5/6&4/3\end{pmatrix},}
and subtracting from #{A} gives the displayed precision.
Its diagonal entries differ by #{1/4}, so its marginal also fails
swap invariance. Its positive Gaussian still exists.}

\p{\citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective}
gives the edge-once convention.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex}
gives the [positive Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D) convention.
The action difference and matrix products are direct calculations
with these conventions.
Weight preservation is sufficient in the general
[transport theorem](fdip-003D); it need not be necessary for every
presentation with redundant loops or parallel edges. This example
establishes failure for the stated weights, not a universal necessity.}
